COORD=r'''Fix $N=\{1,\ldots,n\}$, a model $v:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$, an input $x$ and one coordinate baseline $r$. Write $g(A)=v(x_A)$ and $b=g(\varnothing)$. Repeated masking defines $g_T(A)=v((x_T)_A)=g(T\cap A)$. Define $I_g(S)=\sum_{L\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|L|}g(L)$ and $w_A=I_g(A)$. For nonempty $S$, define $O_g^N(S)=-I_{g^c}(S)$ with $g^c(L)=g(N\setminus L)$; separately set $O_g^N(\varnothing)=g(\varnothing)$. All subset sums include the empty set, and raw outputs need not have zero baseline. For components, use $v_{and}$ and $v_{or}$ to form their respective games and conditional games.'''
LABEL=COORD+r''' Pages 4 and 6 allow arbitrary mask-label parameters $\gamma_A$. Thus the most general joint decomposition consists of $g_{and}(A)=\tfrac12g(A)+\gamma_A$ and $g_{or}(A)=\tfrac12g(A)-\gamma_A$, or any two set functions summing to $g(A)$. Define $g_{and,T}(L)=g_{and}(T\cap L)$ and similarly for OR. Here $v_{and}(x_A),v_{or}(x_A)$ denote mask-label values; they are actual evaluations on input vectors only when the components come from input functions. The case $x_i=r_i$ is allowed. Distinct labels need not give distinct inputs, and arbitrary gamma values need not extend to a single-valued input function.'''
ASSUMPTIONS={
'finite':['Finite $N$; the same fixed coordinate baseline $r$, input $x$ and model throughout. No zero-baseline assumption on raw outputs.'],
'label':[r'$g(U)=v(x_U)$; finite $N$ and $T\subseteq N$. AND and OR components are arbitrary set functions, with $g(U)=g_{and}(U)+g_{or}(U)$ for every label $U$. No assumption $x_i\ne r_i$ or extension to input functions is required.'],
'noise':['Fixed model, input, baseline and unperturbed interaction.',r'Appendix D assumes all output perturbations are IID $N(0,\sigma^2)$.','Variance is taken over the joint noise sample; adaptively optimized gamma is not treated as fixed.']}
STEPS={
'f11-mask-comp':('Complete mask-composition lemma',r'Fix a coordinate $i$. If $i\in T\cap L$, both masks retain $x_i$. If $i\notin L$, the second sets it to $r_i$. If $i\in L\setminus T$, the first has already set it to $r_i$ and the second retains that value. Hence $(x_T)_L=x_{T\cap L}$ coordinatewise. Both masks must use the same $r$.'),
'f11-mask-zero-pair':('Choose a removed variable and pair subsets',r'If $S\not\subseteq T$, choose $i\in S\setminus T$. For each $U\subseteq S\setminus\{i\}$, we have $T\cap(U\cup\{i\})=T\cap U$. The corresponding conditional outputs are equal and their signs are opposite, so every pair cancels. Neither linearity of the model nor $g(\varnothing)=0$ is required.'),
'f11-mask-boundary':('Empty-coalition and unmasked boundaries',r'The empty set is always contained in $T$, so the vanishing statement excludes the empty interaction, whose value is $b$. For $T=N$ there is no $S\not\subseteq T$. For $T=\varnothing$, every nonempty conditional interaction vanishes.'),
'f11-dual-game':('Define the game with reversed states',r'Exchanging retain and baseline states gives $g^c(L)=g(N\setminus L)$. This reverses the finite state indices; it does not replace the model with another model.'),
'f11-dual-definition':('Compare the nonempty OR and AND definitions term by term',r'For $S\ne\varnothing$, the OR terms form the AND transform of $g^c$ with an overall minus sign. Values are generally opposite, with equal magnitude. Empty OR is separately $g(\varnothing)$, whereas negative empty AND of $g^c$ is $-g(N)$; these need not agree.'),
'f11-dual-example':('Two-variable and empty-coalition example',r'Take $N=\{1,2\}$ and outputs $g(\varnothing)=7$, $g(\{1\})=8$, $g(\{2\})=9$, $g(N)=13$. The raw AND coefficients are $7,1,2,3$. The complement game has singleton AND values $-4,-5$ and pair value $3$, giving OR values $4,5,-3$ with empty baseline $7$. Its reversed empty AND value is $13$; its negative $-13$ cannot replace $7$.'),
'f11-exact-adapt':('Adapt the raw AND definition',r'With $x,r$ fixed, the paper coefficient $I_{and}(A\mid x)$ is $I_g(A)$. It uses raw outputs and has empty value $b$.'),
'f11-parent-decompose':('Use the original decomposition at each mask label',r'The original decomposition satisfies $g(U)=g_{and}(U)+g_{or}(U)$ at every label. In the arbitrary gamma parameterization, the two gamma terms cancel immediately. Even when $x_i=r_i$ makes two labels yield the same vector, the components remain valid set functions; no extension-to-input-function condition is added.'),
'f11-and-coeff-align':('Align fixed-input and literal conditional coefficients',r'If $S\subseteq T$, then every $L\subseteq S$ also lies in $T$, so $T\cap L=L$. Thus $g_{and,T}(L)=g_{and}(L)$ at each summand, and $I_{g_{and,T}}(S)=I_{g_{and}}(S)$. For actual input-function components, $(x_T)_L=x_L$ gives the same alignment.'),
'f11-and-reconstruct':('Apply complete finite reconstruction and check its conditions',r'Apply finite reconstruction to $g_{and,T}$: the sum over $S\subseteq T$ is $g_{and,T}(T)=g_{and}(T)$ since $T\cap T=T$. The preceding alignment gives the same value using fixed-input coefficients. Both sums include $g_{and}(\varnothing)$; no zero-baseline condition is added.'),
'f11-and-empty':('Empty-mask boundary',r'For $T=\varnothing$, the only summand is $S=\varnothing$ and both sides equal $g_{and}(\varnothing)$. This holds for arbitrary label components and actual input-function components.'),
'f11-or-literal-game':('Retain the literal conditional input and correct the original substitution',r'Fix $T\subseteq N$ and set $h(L)=g_{or,T}(L)=g_{or}(T\cap L)$, retaining the conditional labels of the parent formula. Then $h(N\setminus L)=g_{or}(T\setminus L)$. Only for input-function components can this be written as $v_{or}((x_T)_{N\setminus L})=v_{or}(x_{T\setminus L})$. Page 13 instead substitutes $v_{or}(x_{N\setminus L})$, which is generally different. The corrected proof covers arbitrary label functions without an extension assumption.'),
'f11-or-filter':('Complete lemma: intersecting subsets form a difference of two families',r'For $S\subseteq N$, $S\cap T=\varnothing$ exactly when $S\subseteq N\setminus T$. Therefore the intersecting family is $\mathcal P(N)\setminus\mathcal P(N\setminus T)$. The latter is contained in the former, so its finite sum is the difference of the two complete sums. The empty set belongs to both complete families and never to the intersecting family.'),
'f11-or-transform':('Reconstruct the negative complement game twice',r'Set $f(L)=-h(N\setminus L)$. For nonempty $S$, finite-sum linearity gives $O_h^N(S)=I_f(S)$. The intersecting family excludes the empty set, so substitution is valid there. Two reconstructions give $f(N)-f(N\setminus T)=-h(\varnothing)+h(T)$, using $N\setminus(N\setminus T)=T$. Finally $h(T)=g_{or}(T)$ and $h(\varnothing)=g_{or}(\varnothing)$.'),
'f11-or-baseline':('Restore the separate empty OR baseline',r'The empty definition is $O_h^N(\varnothing)=h(\varnothing)=g_{or}(\varnothing)$. Restoring it reconstructs $g_{or}(T)$. For $T=\varnothing$, the intersecting family is empty and only the baseline remains. For $T=N$, it reconstructs the full label value. This includes the coincident empty-mask boundaries of the original cases.'),
'f11-parent-add':('Add the components to obtain the literal parent formula',r'The AND and OR parts reconstruct $g_{and}(T)$ and $g_{or}(T)$. Add them and use the original decomposition to get $g(T)=v(x_T)$. The two empty terms sum to $g(\varnothing)$. Coefficients still come from $g_{and,T},g_{or,T}$; input-function components are a special case.'),
'f11-or-example':('Two-variable check with a nonzero baseline',r'For outputs $7,8,9,13$ on $\varnothing,\{1\},\{2\},N$, raw AND coefficients are $7,1,2,3$ and fixed-input OR coefficients are $4,5,-3$ with empty value $7$. For the literal $T=\{1\}$ formula, use $h_T(L)=g(T\cap L)$ with outputs $7,8,7,8$. Its OR coefficients are $1,0,0$, with empty $7$. The intersecting sum gives $7+1+0=8=g(T)$. The fixed-input formula also gives $7+4-3=8$, but its individual coefficients differ. Empty $T$ gives baseline $7$; for $T=N$, the games agree and give $7+4+5-3=13$.'),
'f11-boolean-truth':('Complete two-variable OR identity',r'View Boolean variables as real values $0,1$. At $(x_4,x_5)=(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)$, the expression $x_4+x_5-x_4x_5$ has values $0,1,1,1$, exactly Boolean OR.'),
'f11-boolean-expand':('Retain the three common AND terms and expand OR',r'Substitute this identity into the original five-variable function. The three AND terms remain, giving six distinct sets $\{1,2,3\},\{2,3\},\{3,4\},\{4\},\{5\},\{4,5\}$. The last coefficient is $-1$ and the others are $+1$. The other decomposition retains three AND terms and one OR term on $\{4,5\}$. They agree pointwise; uniqueness or global optimality is not asserted.'),
'f11-boolean-support':('Actual supports at the full input',r'At the all-one input with zero baseline, the six product terms are pure AND functions; shared distribution and linearity determine their coefficients. The alternative uses the same three AND supports and one nonempty OR coefficient $1$ on $\{4,5\}$. This OR property follows since its complement game is constant $1$ minus pure AND. Both expressions are $0$ at the all-zero or empty input and $4$ at the full input.'),
'f11-count-bijection':('Two states per variable are in bijection with subsets',r'Each $i\in N$ chooses retained input or baseline. Its retained-coordinate set $S$ determines the setting uniquely, and every subset determines a setting. Thus there are $2^n$ settings. Even if $x_i=r_i$ makes distinct settings yield the same vector, their number is still $2^n$.'),
'f11-count-queries':('Query count for complete enumeration',r'Querying the model once per setting uses $2^n$ calls, or $m2^n$ for $m$ models enumerated once each. This counts a query scheme; it alone does not bound total interaction transforms, training or gradient-optimization time.'),
'f11-count-boundary':('Zero variables and the six-variable example',r'For $n=0$, there is one empty setting, $1=2^0$. For $n=6$, there are $64$. The timing table is experimental; setting counts do not determine elapsed seconds.'),
'f11-shapley-component':('Check that these are original-model AND interactions',r'Theorem 3 uses AND interactions from the original model $v$, not coefficients of an arbitrary learned $v_{and}$ component. Set $g(A)=v(x_A)$ and $i\in N$ to satisfy the classical factorial-Shapley and shared-proof premises.'),
'f11-shapley-reindex':('Reindex environments by coalitions containing the variable',r'Environment subsets $U\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}$ correspond bijectively to coalitions containing $i$ by $S=U\cup\{i\}$, with inverse $U=S\setminus\{i\}$ and $|S|=|U|+1$. Termwise reindexing yields the original denominator $|S|$. The empty set contains no $i$ and receives no allocation.')}
STEPS.update({
'f11-external-sparsity-step-1':('Scope of the external citation',r'Page 3 and Appendix B on page 12 restate sparsity. The first condition requires every output mixed derivative above order $M$ to vanish throughout the input domain (Sparse assumption 1beta); the implication 1beta to 1alpha gives exact high-order interaction vanishing. The remaining conditions are monotonicity of mean output and a polynomial lower bound on the mean masked output. This paper does not re-prove a quantified sparsity rate from them. Sparse Theorems 2 and 3 and their Case 1 scope remain external dependencies.'),
'f11-external-sparsity-step-2':('Boundary of the available conclusion','The empirical term well-trained cannot be replaced with a new mathematical predicate carrying a stronger sufficient sparsity assumption. A finite-n counting bound from Sparse also cannot be upgraded to a strict asymptotic claim for every network. This entry explains the sources and conditions; the paper has no independent proof.'),
'f11-theorem1-approx-step-1':('Separate exact reconstruction from approximation',r'The exact full-interaction clause of Theorem 1 follows from the complete shared finite Mobius identity. The small-salient-family approximation cites Sparse theory and empirical sparsity without a uniform tolerance. The approximate sum includes the empty interaction exactly when it belongs to the retained family; an extra baseline cannot be added unconditionally. Exact reconstruction makes the residual the sum of all omitted coefficients, including the empty one if omitted, but does not bound that sum.'),
'f11-theorem1-approx-step-2':('Meaning of approximation','The paper supplies neither an epsilon nor a uniform error bound, and does not quantify few salient. This project adds no error premise and does not replace the clause with a threshold-error theorem. Empirical matching plots and rigorous external bounds remain separate entries.'),
'f11-proposition1-step-1':('Exact matching with all interactions',r'The literal conditional input $x_T$ must be retained. With the same model components and baseline, define $g^T(L)=v(x_{T\cap L})$ and the corresponding AND/OR component games. Repeated masking gives this intersection identity. Their raw transforms on the same $N$ are the original $I(S\mid x_T)$. AND uses $S\subseteq T$; OR uses $S\cap T\ne\varnothing$ plus its separate empty term. The component baselines sum to $v(x_\varnothing)$ and their values at $T$ sum to $v(x_T)$, proving literal Theorem 2. Conditional OR coefficients cannot be replaced termwise with unmasked-input coefficients.'),
'f11-proposition1-step-2':('The actual remainder after salient-family truncation',r'Remove coefficients outside the retained AND and OR families. The error is exactly the two omitted conditional-interaction sums, retaining $I^T$ for each original conditional input. The paper asks for both families to be much smaller than $2^n$ and approximate all masks but supplies no independent quantitative derivation. For arbitrary decompositions, exact reconstruction does not itself make either remainder small.'),
'f11-reparameterization-step-1':('From parameters to a decomposition',r'Fix the input, model and baseline, and choose any real family $\gamma_T$. At each $T$, the positive and negative gamma terms cancel, so the two components sum to $g(T)$, including the empty mask and the uncentered baseline.'),
'f11-reparameterization-step-2':('Recover the unique parameter from a decomposition',r'Given any $a(T)+b(T)=g(T)$, set $\gamma_T=(a(T)-b(T))/2$. Substitution gives $a=g/2+\gamma$ and $b=g/2-\gamma$. Any parameter satisfying the first equation equals $a-g/2$, so it is unique. Pointwise reasoning over all masks proves the bijection of set functions without assuming optimization succeeds.'),
'f11-reparameterization-step-3':('Adapt independently to multiple models',r'Apply the same argument to each model $i$, obtaining its own $\gamma_T^{(i)}$ and components. These parameters are model-specific, distinct from the later shared gamma constraint.'),
'f11-rowmax-penalty-step-1':('The original row norm',r'Page 6 defines row infinity norm, so the row penalty is $R=\max_i|a_i|\ge0$. Suppose model $j$ retains magnitude $R$ while other magnitudes change within $R$. The new maximum is at most $R$; the retained $j$ term also makes it at least $R$.'),
'f11-rowmax-penalty-step-2':('Local invariance of the total penalty',r'If only this row changes, its rowmax coordinate remains unchanged, so the sum of absolute rowmax coordinates is unchanged. This is the precise meaning of other models without a penalty: other values in the row may change within magnitude $R$, with either sign.'),
'f11-rowmax-penalty-step-3':('Boundary of the optimization interpretation','The paper interprets the objective as encouraging a shared sparse family. This flat-region property neither proves every optimum has the same support nor guarantees that learned interactions generalize across models. No such global conclusion is added.'),
'f11-and-variance-step-1':('The actual random-variable identity',r'Appendix D writes the noisy interaction as the fixed interaction plus $\sum_{L\subseteq T}c_L\epsilon_L$, where $c_L=(-1)^{|T|-|L|}$. The fixed interaction is constant, so translation does not change variance. Gaussian terms have finite second moments, as do their deterministic sign changes.'),
'f11-and-variance-step-2':('Independence and additivity of variance',r'Distinct output noises are independent by the original IID assumption. Multiplication by fixed signs preserves independence. The variance of a finite independent square-integrable sum is the sum of variances; this does not assume the conclusion. Each sign has square one, so each term contributes $\sigma^2$.'),
'f11-and-variance-step-3':('Count subsets and correct the source notation',r'There are $2^{|T|}$ subsets of $T$, giving the claimed variance. For $T=\varnothing$, raw AND is the noisy output baseline with variance $\sigma^2$, consistent with the formula. The first sentence on page 15 mixes an $S$-indexed centering sum and a single-noise $T$ index. The actual same-$T$ variance follows the original proof, while that sentence is recorded separately as an issue.'),
'f11-or-variance-step-1':('Expand the actual nonempty OR random variable',r'For nonempty $T\subseteq N$, OR is the negative Mobius transform of complement outputs. Perturbing each fixed $g_{or}(L)$ by $\epsilon_L$ and distributing the finite sum gives the original interaction minus $\sum_{L\subseteq T}(-1)^{|T|-|L|}\epsilon_{N\setminus L}$. This identity comes from the definition and is not imposed as the target premise.'),
'f11-or-variance-step-2':('Complement indices preserve independence',r'For $L,K\subseteq T\subseteq N$, equality $N\setminus L=N\setminus K$ implies $L=K$ by complementing in the same universe. Thus distinct $L$ use distinct IID outputs; the reindexed family remains independent and identically distributed. The deterministic negative signs also square to one. The finite Gaussian variance sum gives $2^{|T|}\sigma^2$.'),
'f11-or-variance-step-3':('The original empty-coalition branch',r'Generalizable separately defines $I_{or}(\varnothing)=v_{or}(x_\varnothing)$. Its noisy baseline has variance $\sigma^2=2^0\sigma^2$. This branch cannot be replaced by the nonempty negative-Mobius formula and differs from Sparse centered empty interaction zero.'),
'f11-equation10-step-1':('A pointwise counterexample',r'Page 6 defines rowmax as $\max(|a|,|b|)$, whereas the Eq. (10) expansion on page 16 uses $|\max(a,b)|$. At $a=-3,b=2$, these are $3$ and $2$, so the row identity fails. Invertibility of the Mobius transform allows such a two-model AND row to be realized by corresponding output decompositions; the values are valid interactions.'),
'f11-equation10-step-2':('Impact and the additional index errors',r'The counterexample refutes only the pointwise row identity used in the expansion. A single point does not show that two optimization minima differ. The free $S$ index and fixed $x_{T_k},\gamma_{T_k}$ inside the $T$ sum remain recorded as printing issues. The parent equation with minima has not been fully assessed while those indices remain unresolved. Replacing the original signed maximum with an absolute maximum would change the statement and cannot certify it.'),
'f11-alpha-zero-step-1':('Original objective definitions',r'Let $P(A,B)$ be the sum of the two rowmax one-norms and $Q(A,B)$ the two entrywise one-norms. The pointwise Eq. (6) objective is $P+\alpha Q$. Substituting $\alpha=0$ gives $P+0Q=P$, the Eq. (5) objective.'),
'f11-alpha-zero-step-2':('Adapt the minimization problem','The feasible parameter domain is the same, so identical pointwise objective functions give the same minimization problem. This says nothing about generalization curves at other alpha values. The empirical ablation on pages 17-18 is separate. The alpha-zero sentence occurs on formal PDF page 17, with its figure continuing on page 18.')})
