SOURCE_EXTRA={
'f11-objectives':r'Both vectors in Eq. (4) contain all $2^n$ interactions. In Eqs. (5) and (6), rows represent common subsets and columns represent models. Rowmax is the row infinity norm, the maximum absolute entry. The one-norm here sums absolute values of all entries, rather than being an induced matrix norm. Eq. (6) uses $\alpha\in[0,1]$. These define objectives; the interpretations about generalization and shortcuts supply no mathematical training guarantee.',
'f11-procedure-example':r'''Appendix L gives eight steps. (1) Use the six tokens in A stitch in time saves nine, with token embeddings in $\mathbb R^{768}$ for BERTBASE or $\mathbb R^{1024}$ for BERTLARGE. (2) Choose each model special-token embedding as its baseline. (3) Enumerate all $2^6=64$ masks, from $T_0=\varnothing$ to $T_{63}=N$. (4) Evaluate each masked model output as log odds of the ground-truth class. (5) Set AND and OR components to half the output plus or minus gamma for every model and mask. (6) Compute interactions using Eqs. (1) and (2); although the printed step includes all indices, the OR empty value must still follow the separate Section 2.1 convention. (7) Learn gamma using Eq. (6) and iterate from step 5 to convergence. (8) Select each model salient family using the absolute-interaction threshold. This is an algorithm example, not a proof of convergence or general correctness.''',
'f11-empirical':'Section 3 and Appendices E, H, I, J, K, M, N and O.1 provide datasets, plots and empirical comparisons. Appendix N.1 initializes gamma twice from N(0,1) and reports AND/OR overlaps of 10.90%/16.18% for BERTBASE and 17.84%/20.90% for BERTLARGE. It does not prove these are different global optima. N.2 plots matching error over all masks after removing learnable error. Empirical plots are not additional mathematical proofs.',
'f11-background-selection':r'Appendices E and O.2 select $t<n$ variables to form $N$ and leave unselected variables unchanged. SST-2 uses at most ten semantic tokens; SQuAD masks whole words with all their tokens and selects ten words; MNIST-3 selects eight foreground $3\times3$ patches with a zero-patch baseline. The background and stopword decisions are empirical choices, not proved zero-interaction conditions.'}

STEPS.update({
 'cvpr-recon-main':('Finite double sum and interval cancellation', r'For fixed $S$, expand $w_A$. Every pair $L\subseteq A\subseteq S$ occurs once, so the finite sums may be exchanged. For fixed $L$, use the bijection $B=A\setminus L\subseteq S\setminus L$, with $|A|-|L|=|B|$. If $L\ne S$, choose $i\in S\setminus L$ and pair the sets $B$ according to whether they contain $i$; the signs are opposite and the inner sum vanishes. If $L=S$, only $B=\varnothing$ remains and its coefficient is one. Hence the sum leaves exactly $g(S)$.'),
 'cvpr-recon-empty':('Check the empty coalition and a two-variable example', r'At $S=\varnothing$, only $w_\varnothing=b$ occurs. Take $g(\varnothing)=7,g(\{1\})=8,g(\{2\})=9,g(\{1,2\})=13$. The coefficients are 7,1,2,3; the full-input sum is 13, the empty-input sum is 7, and the singleton sums are 8 and 9.')})

